.Dd October 18, 2026
.Dt CAPSULE.CONF 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm capsule.conf
.Nd configuration file for Capsule
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
file configures the
.Xr capsule 8
daemon.
It uses
UCL format.
The normal installation and daemon default is
.Pa /etc/capsule.conf .
.Pp
Only an absent default configuration file permits compiled-in
defaults; an explicitly selected missing file is an error.
The file must be regular, owned by root or the effective user, not writable by
group or others, and at most 1 MiB.
Symbolic links are rejected.
.Pp
The parser rejects unknown and duplicate keys, invalid types and values,
embedded NUL/control bytes in strings, overlong paths, excess or duplicate
claims, UCL macros and includes.
Variables are not expanded.
A failed load leaves the previous configuration unchanged.
An empty or comment-only file explicitly retains initialized defaults.
Configuration failure aborts daemon startup; PID 1 uses its fatal reboot path
instead of entering a recovery shell with rejected policy.
.Ss General
The following top-level keys are recognized:
.Bl -tag -width "control_socket_mode"
.It Va pidfile
Path to the pidfile.
Default:
.Pa /var/run/capsule.pid .
.It Va control_socket
Path to the control socket.
Default:
.Pa /var/run/capsule.sock .
.It Va control_socket_mode
Quoted octal file mode for the control socket.
Default:
.Dq 0700 .
.It Va service_manager
Path to the first managed process started by
.Xr capsule 8 .
Default:
.Pa /usr/libexec/switchboard .
If set to an empty string, no service manager is started.
Delegated manager descriptors are confined before execution:
they survive the initial exec, are locked against inheritance by further forks,
and close on subsequent execs.
These limits are established before the manager's constructors run.
May be overridden with
.Xr capsule 8 Ns 's
.Fl b
option.
.El
.Ss Integrity
The
.Va integrity
section is an object whose keys are booleans controlling
.Xr mac_capability_capprotect 4
shield flags that protect the
.Xr capsule 8
process from external interference.
.Bl -tag -width "visible"
.It Va ptrace
Block ptrace attach from foreign nonces.
Maps to
.Dv CP_SF_PTRACE .
Default: true.
.It Va signal
Block all signals except
.Dv SIGKILL
and
.Dv SIGCONT
from foreign nonces.
Maps to
.Dv CP_SF_SIGNAL .
Always enabled and cannot be disabled by configuration.
.It Va sigkill
Block
.Dv SIGKILL
from foreign nonces.
Maps to
.Dv CP_SF_SIGKILL .
Always enabled and cannot be disabled by configuration.
.It Va sigcont
Block
.Dv SIGCONT
from foreign nonces.
Maps to
.Dv CP_SF_SIGCONT .
Always enabled and cannot be disabled by configuration.
.It Va visible
Hide
.Xr capsule 8
from
.Xr ps 1 ,
.Xr top 1 ,
and procfs enumeration.
Maps to
.Dv CP_SF_VISIBLE .
Default: false.
.It Va wait
Block
.Xr wait4 2
from non-parent processes.
Maps to
.Dv CP_SF_WAIT .
Default: true.
.It Va sched
Block scheduler priority and CPU affinity manipulation.
Maps to
.Dv CP_SF_SCHED .
Default: true.
.It Va core
Suppress core dumps to prevent leaking secrets from process memory.
Maps to
.Dv CP_SF_CORE .
Default: false.
.It Va ktrace
Block passive syscall tracing via
.Xr ktrace 1 .
Maps to
.Dv CP_SF_KTRACE .
Default: true.
.El
.Pp
Together,
.Va signal ,
.Va sigkill ,
and
.Va sigcont
deny every foreign-nonce ambient PID-based signal, including signal 0.
.Xr service 8
uses the root-only control socket for status, reload, and graceful shutdown.
Forced termination is deliberately reserved for a supervisor holding an
.Xr pdfork 2
process descriptor; signaling through
.Xr pdkill 2
does not depend on ambient PID authority.
.Ss Boot host identity
The optional top-level
.Va hostuuid
is a deployment-supplied, stable UUID in canonical lowercase
.Dq 8-4-4-4-12
hexadecimal notation.
The nil UUID and UUIDs deriving a zero host ID are rejected.
It is required when
.Va storage
is nonempty and is accepted only by the original PID 1 personality.
Before acquiring storage, PID 1 sets and verifies
.Va kern.hostuuid
and
.Va kern.hostid
from this authenticated input.
The host ID follows the existing rc hostid convention: the first four bytes of
MD5 of the UUID text, interpreted in network byte order.
There is no implicit hardware, environment or writable-file fallback.
.Pp
Provision a unique UUID per machine and preserve it across system generations.
The generation must also supply the configured hostid and machine-id
compatibility files for normal startup; see
.Xr rc.conf 5 .
The rc services verify these against the protected kernel identity without
regenerating them.
Changing it requires a new authenticated boot policy and may prevent importing
an unexported pool.
Neither this 32-bit identifier nor the UUID is proof of machine ownership;
cloning them does not provide safe shared-disk or rollback protection.
Host identity, like storage assignments, cannot change on manager restart or
policy reload.
.Ss Boot Crypto control
The optional
.Va crypto_recipient
string names one exact bundle/unit label eligible for a Crypto supervisor
control descriptor.
An absent or empty value grants no Crypto control.
The packaged base policy selects
.Dq Li system.Crypto/bsdcrypto ;
this is policy data, not a recipient compiled into Capsule or SwitchBoard.
The recipient must also be a verified system Core unit.
.Pp
Original PID 1 opens the kernel control endpoint and restricts the delivered
descriptor to introspection and child-control derivation.
SwitchBoard confines it to the launch, and libservice checks its kernel type
and exact descriptor/ioctl rights before the provider can consume it.
Root identity or a matching executable name alone is insufficient.
The field cannot change during reload or manager restart; changing it requires
a new authenticated system generation in BIP mode.
.Pp
This delivery mechanism does not yet provide explicit grant revocation or
complete crash/restart semantics.
Those remain qualification requirements, as does actual provider operation
under enforcing BIP and Secure Boot.
.Ss Boot storage
The
.Va storage
array declares boot-only dataset handle delegation.
An empty array grants no persistent storage.
A nonempty array is accepted only by the original PID 1 personality.
Tables are allocated for the selected entries, subject to a 16 MiB expanded-memory
budget per table and the 1 MiB configuration-file limit.
Reload preserves the previous configuration on parse or allocation failure;
boot storage assignments cannot change on reload.
.Pp
Each entry requires these fields:
.Bl -tag -width "dataset_guid"
.It Va recipient
An exact bundle and unit label, separated by one slash.
.It Va pool
Expected pool name.
.It Va pool_guid
Expected pool GUID as a quoted positive decimal string, without leading zeroes.
.It Va dataset
Expected dataset name, strictly below the named pool.
Pool roots and snapshot names are rejected.
.It Va dataset_guid
Expected dataset GUID in the same format as
.Va pool_guid .
.It Va devices
One through eight explicit character-device paths beneath
.Pa /dev .
Every backing leaf, spare and cache device must match an approved device
identity; duplicate aliases and regular-file-backed pools are rejected.
Disk, mirror, RAID-Z, replacing and spare topologies are supported.
Other topologies are rejected.
.It Va rights
Explicit array of TrustedZFS operation names:
.Dq props_read , props_write , snapshot , snap_destroy , rollback ,
.Dq clone_src , create , destroy , send , recv , mount , hold , event ,
.Dq scrub , rename , promote , bookmark , release .
The implicit rights
.Dq props_read
and
.Dq event
must be included explicitly.
.El
.Pp
Duplicate recipients, overlapping dataset roots, and conflicting identities or
device lists for the same pool are rejected.
PID 1 acquires the configured anchors before starting the service manager.
It imports only the explicitly selected pool, without mounting datasets,
writing a cache file, forcing import or rewinding.
Unexported pools require a nonzero matching kernel host ID;
.Dv ZFS_HOSTID
does not override this decision.
Multihost state must permit import.
A pool imported before a later validation failure may remain imported, but no
service receives a handle and bootstrap fails.
.Pp
Anchors are published only after every acquisition and descriptor restriction
succeeds. They cannot be transferred or inherited across fork or exec.
The service manager receives bounded derivatives for eligible recipients through
the existing storage-root bootstrap resource. Manager restarts preserve anchors.
Ordinary system startup remains responsible for named filesystem mounts.
.Pp
The removed BIP keys
.Va attachment , content , management , data ,
and
.Va policy
are rejected. Storage delegation does not provide BIP protection.
.Ss Claims
The
.Va claims
section lists resources to be claimed exclusively by
.Xr capsule 8
at boot via the
.Xr mac_capability_isolation 4
service.
Once claimed, MACF hooks deny access from all other processes,
including root.
.Pa /dev/mac_capability
is always claimed automatically regardless of configuration; it is the
only path
.Xr capsule 8
claims.
Filesystem paths are brokered by
.Xr bsdfilesystem 8 ,
which opens a path and returns a rights-limited descriptor, so no
.Va paths
claim exists.
.Pp
Claims declared here are
.Em policy claims
.Pq Dv CLAIM_SOURCE_POLICY .
They are acquired at boot, survive
.Xr switchboard 8
restarts, and cannot be released via the channel.
.Pp
Resources requested by services but not listed here are auto-claimed
dynamically at runtime with reference counting.
These
.Em dynamic claims
are released from the kernel when no service needs them.
.Xr capsulectl 8
.Cm status
shows
.Ql [policy]
for manifest claims and
.Ql [service, refcount=N]
for dynamic claims.
.Pp
Claim limits: at most 32 network claims.
.Bl -tag -width "network"
.It Va network
Array of network endpoint objects to claim.
Each object supports the following keys:
.Bl -tag -width "direction"
.It Va port Pq or Va ports
Port number, range, or wildcard.
May be an integer, a range string
.Pq e.g., Dq 80-8000 ,
.Dq *
for all ports (0\(en65535), or
.Dq .1024
or
.Dq <1024
for privileged ports (0\(en1023).
Optional; default is all ports (0\(en65535).
.Va ports
is accepted as an alias for
.Va port .
Specifying both is an error.
Range endpoints must contain decimal digits; an omitted endpoint is invalid.
.It Va protocol
.Dq tcp ,
.Dq udp ,
the Bluetooth socket protocols
.Dq l2cap ,
.Dq rfcomm ,
.Dq sco ,
.Dq iso ,
.Dq hci ,
.Dq any ,
or
.Dq * .
Default:
.Dq any .
.It Va direction
.Dq bind ,
.Dq connect ,
.Dq any ,
or
.Dq * .
Default:
.Dq bind .
.It Va domain
.Dq inet ,
.Dq inet6 ,
.Dq bluetooth ,
.Dq any ,
or
.Dq * .
Default:
.Dq inet .
A
.Dq bluetooth
claim reserves the
.Dv AF_BLUETOOTH
socket family; a fully-wildcard bluetooth claim
.Pq protocol, address, and direction all
.Dq any
reserves the entire radio to the claiming nonce, so that every other
process is denied at
.Xr socket 2
create time across HCI, L2CAP, RFCOMM, SCO, and ISO.
.It Va address
For
.Dq inet
and
.Dq inet6 ,
an IP address or CIDR prefix.
An IPv4 or IPv6 address without a prefix length matches a single host.
CIDR notation
.Pq e.g., Dq 192.0.2.0/24 No or Dq 2001:db8::/32
matches a network.
For
.Dq bluetooth ,
a device address of the form
.Dq aa:bb:cc:dd:ee:ff
matches a single peer.
.Dq *
matches any address.
Optional; if omitted, any address is matched.
A supplied CIDR slash requires a numeric prefix length.
An explicit domain must match the address family.
If domain is omitted, the address selects its family.
.El
.Pp
Foreign nonces receive
.Er EACCES
on bind or connect to claimed endpoints.
Default: empty.
.It Va system
Array of system gate names to claim via the
.Xr mac_capability_system 4
service.
Once claimed, the gated operation is denied to all processes
unless they hold an authorized token from
.Xr capsule 8 .
Available gate names:
.Dq kldload ,
.Dq kldunload ,
.Dq reboot ,
.Dq swapon ,
.Dq swapoff ,
.Dq sysctl ,
.Dq kenv ,
.Dq kenv_read ,
.Dq acct ,
.Dq audit .
Default: empty.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Minimal configuration relying on compiled-in defaults:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
pidfile = "/var/run/capsule.pid";
control_socket = "/var/run/capsule.sock";
service_manager = "/usr/libexec/switchboard";
.Ed
.Pp
Full integrity hardening.
Note that enabling
.Va signal
prevents
.Xr rc 8
from sending signals to
.Xr capsule 8 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
integrity {
    ptrace = true;
    signal = true;
    visible = true;
    wait = true;
    sched = true;
    core = true;
    ktrace = true;
}
.Ed
.Pp
Claim a privileged port range on a specific subnet and system
operations:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
claims {
    network = [
        {
            port = "<1024";
            protocol = "tcp";
            direction = "bind";
            domain = "inet";
            address = "192.0.2.0/24";
        },
        {
            port = 8443;
            protocol = "tcp";
            direction = "bind";
            domain = "inet6";
            address = "2001:db8::/32";
        },
    ];
    system = ["kldload", "kldunload", "reboot", "sysctl"];
}
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr mac_capability 4 ,
.Xr mac_capability_capprotect 4 ,
.Xr mac_capability_isolation 4 ,
.Xr mac_capability_system 4 ,
.Xr bsdfilesystem 8 ,
.Xr capsule 8 ,
.Xr capsulectl 8 ,
.Xr switchboard 8
